1
00:00:00,720 --> 00:03:32,430
The rope has no power to bind, because the rope itself is the Lord's energy. He has no inside and no outside, no beginning and no end. He is before this world and after it. The world can be destroyed, and He remains. He is beyond the limit of the world, and He is inside it too. A spark taken from a great fire cannot set that fire burning. In the same way His own energy cannot bind Him. He is outside and inside the universe, and He is the universe. Thinking Him her own child, she bound Him. Here atma means the body. Mother had only one conviction: this is my child. She had seen the whole universe in His mouth, and she had forgotten it. That Lord, appearing in a human form and remaining beyond the senses, a gopi bound to the mortar, as an ordinary child is bound. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.13-14]

2
00:03:32,430 --> 00:05:47,170
Two years earlier, in a house abroad, children and relatives had come. They did not understand Hindi and were making a noise. Someone shut them all in a small room. The children thought the police might come. They cried loudly. They became sure they would be left shut, and that no one would open the door. At last they were let out. An hour of the katha was finished. They were ordinary children, shut in a room. The Lord was bound in the same way. Goswami Tulsidas, in the Vinaya-patrika, says that everyone is bound by the fruit of his own work. Upon that same Lord, the Supreme, a binding was placed that He would not open by His own power. There was neither the thought nor the courage. If He opened it, the stick would fall. Balarama did not open it. No friend opened it. Everyone asked who had bound Him. Yashoda had bound Him. Then who would untie Him? No one had the courage.

3
00:05:48,460 --> 00:06:43,020
Sridhara Swami says the Lord shows that in Himself there is neither bondage nor liberation. He is eternally free. Others bind Him, and others release Him. In truth He was bound by the rope of love. He tried hard not to be bound. First He tried not to be caught, and He was caught. Then He tried not to let the rope fall, and the rope fell too. The Lord loses to the loving insistence of His devotee. To be defeated is His nature. Shukadeva will say ahead that by this pastime the Lord shows that He is controlled by His devotee.

4
00:06:43,530 --> 00:10:57,020
What happened while she was binding Him, Shukadeva says again. As the rope was being tied on that little offender, it was short by two fingers, so the gopi joined another. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.15] Again and again the word gopi comes. Go is also used for the earth, and gopi is used to speak Mother Yashoda's vatsalya. The wealth of the house, the means of its keeping, this child was spoiling. The first rope was short by two fingers. She did not become disturbed. She joined another. The shortage was two fingers, so she may have added about a hand's length. When that too was short, she joined yet another. Whatever rope she took up for the binding was short by two fingers. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.16] The second was short. She added a third, and it was short by two fingers again. Then she began joining long ropes. She took whatever rope her hand could reach. She ordered the gopis and the maids who were standing there, bring rope, why are you only watching. The gopis were astonished. What has happened to the queen. If the binding will not hold, she should let it go. The rope did not look short, and the waist was not growing thick, and still, the moment she went to knot it, two fingers were gone.

5
00:10:58,620 --> 00:14:36,280
In this way Yashoda joined the ropes of her own house, and as the gopis smiled, she too smiled, and she was astonished. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.17] First she joined the ropes of her own house. When those would not bind Him, she began sending for ropes from other houses. A singer has sung, no rope could be found in the town of Gokula. People kept bringing them. With delight she ordered, bring more, bring more. The gopis were smiling. Susmayantinam. Here su means clear, open. They were glad that Lala was not coming into the binding. Mother wanted to teach Him, and the binding would not hold. The child was sobbing. The same mother, who loves Him so much, wants to bind Him today. They were smiling at her failure. When a person is deep in a task and another laughs, it hurts. They were laughing openly. What has happened to the queen. What magic is this. Mother Yashoda was not greatly troubled. She too was smiling, and she was amazed. Why is the rope shrinking. This is the child's power, a power beyond this world, and she did not understand it. Very long ropes were being joined, and they were not enough. Sending for them again and again, joining the knots, wrapping them at the waist, took a long time. Still she said, no, I will bind Him.

6
00:14:37,680 --> 00:19:06,300
Because of this, Mother's body was soaked with perspiration. She had run after Krishna through the village, and now she was making knot after knot. Seeing His mother, her body wet and the garland of her hair fallen loose, Krishna saw her labor and, out of mercy, agreed to be bound. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.18] Svinna means a body wet with sweat. First she churned, then she ran, and now the knots. The binding of her hair opened and the hair scattered. As Yashoda tried to bind Him, the eternal energies that belong to the Lord's own form stood ready. All-pervading power, the power of opulence, the power of knowing everything. We will not let the unlimited, the boundless, the Supreme Brahman be bound. There was a race among the energies. But the Lord saw that Mother was soaked with sweat and her hair had come loose. Seeing her labor, He was merciful, and He came into His own binding. The knot closed, at the waist. Then Mother was at peace. She thought, stay like this a little while. The butter has to be taken out, the yogurt has to be churned. Let me finish the work, then I will soothe Him, take Him on my lap, and feed Him. Sva-bandhane means the binding of His own people, His mother's binding. The two fingers were short because the mercy was not yet complete. One finger was Mother's labor. When that was complete and the mercy was complete, the knot closed. The rope was tied at the waist, and so He is Damodara. Dama means the rope, and udara means the waist, the belly.

7
00:19:07,960 --> 00:22:14,830
O King, by this the Lord showed that He is controlled by His servant. Krishna, by whose will this universe and its lords are controlled, is Himself controlled. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.19] The whole world is in Krishna's control, and so are Brahma, Shiva, and the demigods. That same Lord stays in the control of His servant. By running, by being caught, and by accepting the rope, the Lord clearly showed this. Remaining under the love of the devotees, He eats the food they give and deals with them in love. For one who wants to understand the Lord, His mind, His relation with the living being, and how He will deal with a person who worships Him, this pastime is the greatest preaching.

8
00:22:16,400 --> 00:25:37,850
If the Lord never spoke, never laughed, had no form, and neither ate nor drank, who would love a Lord heard of in that way. So He shows His real nature. See how I am in the devotee's control. One who thinks Me a son, I am his son. Mother Yashoda is so worried that her boy should be well behaved and intelligent. For that she does not hesitate to scold or to bind. The child has no sense of what He should eat and what He should not break. This is what the Lord is showing. Shukadeva is pointing this out to those who read and hear the Bhagavatam. Love such a Lord, who accepts this control. A person who is great and makes himself small becomes dear to everyone. One who only displays greatness is loved by few. Prahlada Maharaj says He is like the sky. He is unlimited in every quality, and still He is the most easily reached. The sky is the greatest thing, and wherever we are, the sky is there. One purpose of His appearance is to show this nature among people, so that they understand Krishna as He is. This is the instruction.

9
00:25:37,980 --> 00:29:57,400
In Chaitanya-charitamrita it is said: wandering and wandering, if the soul finds a sadhu who is the physician, then by the mantra of his instruction the witch flees. [Chaitanya-charitamrita, Madhya 22.14] Maya is called a witch. The soul is in her control. If, while wandering, he meets the sadhu physician, the mantra of that instruction drives the witch away. This mantra of instruction is Srimad-Bhagavatam. Each verse is a mantra. Speaking one or two mantras does not remove the hold of maya. The words of scripture, filled with the Lord's pastimes, are the mantra. By hearing, the hold of maya lessens. When a person becomes greedy for Krishna's qualities, the insistence on this world falls away. I do this, I enjoy this, this is mine: he leaves that feeling. Kings who lacked nothing left their kingdoms and went to the forest. If we truly try to hear the Bhagavatam, the influence of maya withdraws on its own. The rising of the desire to hear is rare. Religion free of cheating is here, the supreme religion of the saints who are free of envy. Here the real object is to be known, which gives auspiciousness and uproots the three miseries. In the Bhagavatam, composed by the great sage, what need is there of anything else? The Lord is at once captured in the heart by those who have acted well and who desire to hear, from that very moment. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 1.1.2] When there has been pious work through many births, the longing to hear rises. From that moment, and at once. One who desires to hear, in that very moment the Lord is captured in his heart. The longing to hear is that rare. If He is captured when the desire merely arises, then when the hearing is complete He will never leave the heart.

10
00:30:00,800 --> 00:31:55,560
In India there were mahatmas who, when they reached this passage, spoke the pastime of the binding rope for many days together. I heard this in Vrindavan itself. Even in an ordinary series it would go on for fifteen days, two hours each day. When the Lord showed His form there, a warrior like Arjuna began to tremble. Here the Lord is trembling, crying, helpless. These pastimes have very great importance. If a day is given only to philosophy, people are not drawn as much. They are drawn by these pastimes.

11
00:32:00,880 --> 00:36:26,070
Maharaj Parikshit is, as it were, asking: had He not shown this before? Is there no one else with Yashoda's fortune and Yashoda's strong love? Shukadeva himself says: neither Brahma, nor Shiva, nor even Lakshmi, who rests on His body, obtained the mercy that the gopi obtained from the giver of liberation. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.20] Lebhire is plural. Saying na three times means they did not obtain it, they did not obtain it, they did not obtain it. The mercy the gopi obtained, the mercy Yashoda obtained, these did not obtain. From Krishna, the giver of love. Mother would put a small piece of fresh bread in His mouth and say, eat it quickly or I will slap You. Out of fear He would eat. Sometimes He would spit it out, as children do. Sometimes she would say, if You drink milk, Your hair will grow as long as Balarama's braid. Krishna would drink and feel it with His hand. It is not growing. Brahma is the crest jewel of all the acharyas. He is the Lord's son and also His disciple. Knowledge was given in his heart, he received the touch of the hand, and he was appointed over creation. He is the father of Narada and the father of the four Kumaras. Still, what was given to Yashoda was not given to Brahma. Shiva, who is held up as the example among Vaishnavas, did not receive this happiness. Nor did Lakshmi.

12
00:36:27,930 --> 00:41:14,840
The greatest mercy is that the Lord comes to the soul as a son, a friend, or a beloved, and the service is done to the heart's content. Feeding, giving drink, singing, swinging the cradle. That Krishna become one of our own family. This is the greatest gift. He gives Himself to the devotees. One who gives his own self is called atmada. He gave Himself to Arjuna by becoming the charioteer. He gave Himself to Bali as the doorkeeper. But as He gave Himself to Yashoda, He gave Himself to no one else. She kept Him in the womb, kept Him on the lap, and feeds Him her own milk. At night Krishna drinks again and again. If a cat is heard, Mother says, no, no, nothing will happen. Devotees themselves become the cat and make the sound, to give Him happiness. He is small, and Mother says, do not go far. Forest was on every side. The childhood forest was eighty-four kos. No creature should eat Him, no snake bite Him, no cow or bull strike Him. One day she said, do not go far, a ghost will come. Krishna asked, what is a ghost? Mother said, it is something frightful. It catches a child and eats him. Then I will never go, and He ran back to her lap. Then He says, Mother, once I took the form of Narasimha, and I did not see a ghost. Mother does not consider it. She does not think, this is the one who became Narasimha. She takes Him on her lap. It is night. Then He says, as Rama I bridged the ocean and killed Ravana. Mother says, yes, yes, You have killed them all, now be quiet and sleep. He crossed the ocean, and He cannot cross the threshold. Then the cat calls, and He clings to her lap in fear. All the avataras are finished. He talks with Yashoda. When she feeds Him, He eats. When she binds Him, He is bound.

13
00:41:14,840 --> 00:45:46,260
Yashoda's fortune is, by connection, everyone's fortune. The friends who play with Him and compete in the games have that happiness too, but among them Yashoda stands highest. Nanda Baba would take Him on his lap and carry Him outside. The performers of the rasa show that when Yashoda bound Him to the mortar, Nanda Baba came to untie Him. Krishna says, now I will be called your son. I will not go to Yashoda's lap. If someone asks, I will say I am Nanda's boy, not Yashoda's. But He has to go at once, because the food is there, and Mother's breast is there. Such talk goes on. The people of Braj are His own. The earth, the trees whose fruit the Lord picked with His own hand and ate, the flowers He picked, this fortune also came to Yashoda and the people of Braj.

14
00:45:47,700 --> 00:50:52,390
The Supreme Lord, the son of the gopi, is not easily obtained by those who identify with the body, by the speculative philosophers, or by those absorbed in the self, as He is obtained by those who have devotion. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.21] It is as if the Lord, lying in the binding of the mortar, is near, and Shukadeva's mind goes beside Him and says this. This son of the gopi, Yashoda's son, cannot be easily obtained by those who hold a body. Not by the jnanis. Not by those absorbed in the self. Dehi here especially means an ascetic who still thinks the body is himself. They perform austerity, they suffer, they fast, and the possessiveness of the body does not leave. They can bear the suffering that nature gives, and the stream of love does not run in the heart. The jnani looks at the body and the real self, the conscious nature, and still he does not love. He too does not obtain Him easily. Chaitanya-charitamrita says that later such a person is born in a Vaishnava family, or he meets a sadhu, and then the pride of austerity and of knowledge goes away. After many births, then he obtains the Lord. Other forms may be obtained. This son of the gopi is not.

15
00:50:53,730 --> 00:55:04,180
Sweetness is the essence of His godhood. This son of the gopi is not obtained by people without devotion. Those who love obtain Him. This verse is very important. All of scripture is joined to it. The goal is the son of the gopi, and the means is devotion. In the world everyone says devotion is the highest, and still the path of knowledge takes hold. When, in this age, even within devotion the chanting of the name is the greatest, why does everyone not chant the name. Meditation, austerity, holy places, the peaks, the Himalaya. If the son of the gopi is to be obtained, devotion must be done. Shukadeva is giving the devotees a great assurance. Whose mind does not long to receive a fortune like Yashoda's. In this body, some receive the Lord's direct company by meditation, and in worship of the deity they receive the fortune of touching His limbs. The perfected body comes when this body is left. From now, that devotion has to be done. Bhaktimatam means to serve in the mind in Mother Yashoda's mood, in the mood of the people of Braj. Those who worship Lakshmipati, or the universal person, or who, though believers, see themselves as not different from the Lord, attain the impersonal, or they go to the Lord's abode as associates. They too are blessed. But this son of the gopi is obtained by devotion, and that devotion follows the mood of Braj. He is the son of the gopi, so He is obtained by understanding oneself as a gopa or a gopi. The word suta has a special weight. This is Shukadeva's own statement. A gopi gave birth to Him. This teaching is sounding here. He is Devaki's son, and at the same time He is Yashoda's son. He is obtained by devotion.

16
00:55:05,780 --> 00:58:37,690
Having said this, Shukadeva goes on. While Mother was absorbed in the affairs of the house, Lord Krishna saw the two arjuna trees, who in a former time had been the guhyakas, the sons of the giver of wealth. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.22] Mother bound Him, but sitting and watching Him was not her only work. There was a great deal of work. Griha-krityeshu is plural. She had no leisure to look at her son's condition. A long time passed. She became absorbed in the housework. The Lord thought, Mother is doing her work, now I must do Mine. Srila Sridhara Swami writes that the bound Lord is becoming the deliverer of others. While He was free and playing, the deliverance of the trees did not come to His mind. When He was bound, mercy came. He saw His own distress, and then He remembered theirs. Otherwise He played hide and seek with the children and stayed in the shade, and He did not think of delivering them. Distress fell, and then He saw that these poor ones were in the birth of trees. A commentary says, in a playful way, that when a person receives suffering, he understands the suffering of another. Adrakshit means He saw, that is, He began to consider. He had not looked before. Two trees, the arjunas, formerly guhyakas, sons of the giver of wealth. In the past, by Narada's curse, they had been made trees, because of intoxication with pride. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.9.23] Maharaj Parikshit asks how a saint like Narada became angry, and what wrong they had done. The answer comes ahead. Here we stop. Hare Krishna. Om namo bhagavate vasudevaya.

17
00:58:37,690 --> 01:01:52,440
Om namo bhagavate vasudevaya. The king said: O Lord, please tell the cause of the curse of those two. What was the condemned act by which darkness came upon the sage of the demigods? [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.10.1] The katha had closed on this, that Mother Yashoda, having bound Krishna to the mortar, became absorbed in the household works. The plural shows she had no leisure to see her son's condition. There was a great deal of housework. Bound to the mortar, the Lord began to consider the twin arjuna trees. They had formerly been the sons of Kuvera, in a demigod birth, and by Narada's curse they had become trees. Madat. They were in fierce pride. Pride of worldly wealth and property. Their names were Nalakuvara and Manigriva. Both were famous, possessed of fortune, extremely rich. Their father is Dhanada, the giver of wealth to others. One name of Kuvera is Dhanada. In heaven, when a very great expenditure is needed, Kuvera provides it. This is described in the Bhagavatam as well. How their two sons lived, and with what luxury, Shukadeva describes a little.

18
01:01:54,780 --> 01:06:50,940
Vigaritam means a condemned act. Narada cannot curse without a cause. By what condemned act did anger come even upon the sage of the demigods? Those who have crossed the modes of nature do not become angry. Narada is beyond the modes, a great devotee, the acharya of all. In his parental love, anger had not come. He gave the curse as a favor. But hearing the word curse, one thinks it is given in anger, and that a boon is given when he is pleased. Maharaj says the Bhakti-rasayana notes that Maharaj Parikshit himself has received a curse, to die in seven days. In his mind it is there: I too made a mistake, and I received a sentence of death. What did Nalakuvara and Manigriva do, that the devarshi was so merciful as to place them in the birth of trees, and that too in Braj? He says with regret, I am about to die from a brahmana's anger. And what auspicious glance of Narada fell, that they became trees assisting the Lord's pastime? Their fortune in the holy abode was very great. The eagerness to hear arose. Great people also make mistakes. The brahmanas and sages of former times were able to curse. They did not carry a weapon in the hand. They spoke a word, and it was fulfilled. So it was said that a brahmana walks over the living beings with a thunderbolt of speech. Great warriors and emperors feared the brahmanas. The brahmanas kept society in order.

19
01:06:52,320 --> 01:10:26,200
Hearing Maharaj Parikshit's question, Shukadeva describes their offense. Having become the attendants of Rudra, the sons of the giver of wealth became very proud. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.10.2] They were the sons of Kuvera, the best among the demigods, born as demigods, and they were acting against their own nature. Being the sons of Dhanada became one cause of pride. The other was that they became the attendants of Rudra, of Lord Shiva. Rudra means one who drives disease away. Even while living near Rudra, their material life did not stop. It increased. They were the sons of a very great person, and they became servants of God, of Rudra. It was a fortune that they received Shiva's service. But they were so rich that even after becoming his attendants, no mood of renunciation or austerity came. Sudriptau. They became exceedingly proud. They are a great man's sons, and they have become servants of the greatest Lord in the world. Now who is there to speak before them. There was no lack of money. The body was supremely beautiful. Shiva's blessing hand was on their heads. Living on Kailasa, they should have been in devotion. Kailasa is a place of devotion to the Lord. Rudra himself stays in devotion. He stays in katha. What were these two doing there? Shukadeva gives two causes of their pride. Attendants of Rudra, and sons of Dhanada.

20
01:10:28,660 --> 01:13:10,380
The place was the beautiful garden of Kailasa, and by the Mandakini they were fierce with pride. This was not one occasion. This was their habit. In the garden of Kailasa they wandered with women and sang. In that holy abode they drank varuni wine. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.10.2] People sin elsewhere and think, if we live by the Ganga or in the Lord's abode, the sin will be cut away. Sin done in another place is destroyed in a holy place. By living, worshiping, and bathing at a tirtha, sins done knowingly and unknowingly are cut away. These two had wealth and power. No one could check them, because Rudra's hand was over them. One meaning of Rudra is also the one who makes the whole world weep, who destroys. Sages used to go to Kailasa, sit with Shiva, and they knew how much disturbance these two were making. Upavana means a planting that Shiva had arranged in a very beautiful way. The Mandakini, the Ganga, was flowing there. On her bank, madotkatau, in very fierce pride. Varuni wine produces the strongest intoxication. It was produced from the churning of the ocean. It is also connected with Varuna. A person who sins will sin even in the holy abode.

21
01:13:11,020 --> 01:18:06,780
Having drunk varuni wine, their eyes rolling with intoxication, they wandered in the flowering forest with women who sang after them. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.10.3] They drank so much that the eyes began to roll. Judgment was finished. Their company was not sadhus or good people. Stri-janaih. Women were their people, their companions. They were wandering in a forest in flower. They would sing, and the women sang along with their song. Anu means after them, and it also means continuously. They did not only drink themselves. They made those women drink. They were goddesses of heaven. They had fallen into bad company. They had not drunk as much, so they were still somewhat conscious. A flowering forest, scented air, a lonely place, this increased the intoxication. Kailasa is the Lord's abode. There, worship is the principal thing. Around Shiva there is no glitter. No compound and no mansion. The atmosphere is beautiful even today. In that abode these two were sinning in this way. The offenses were several. Doing wrong while being Shiva's servants. Sinning in a pure abode like Kailasa. Sinning on the bank of the Ganga, who washes sin away. And the women were helpers in it. In the holy abode one should stay with the Lord's devotees. The atmosphere, the mind, the thought, all in devotion. Having come to the abode, one should not be caught up with women. Shukadeva is not expanding their gestures. There is no need. Everyone can understand. It was not only singing with the women.

22
01:18:06,880 --> 01:22:52,040
They had not gone with a plan to bathe. There was no second cloth. After wandering in the forest came wandering in the water. They took off their cloth and went into the Ganga. One commentator says that while bathing, the cloth came loose and went, and they did not know where. Whatever was forgotten. The taking off of the cloth is not described. The mood had come. The nature of one who plays in the water stays the same in water, in the sky, or on the earth. He stays in sin. Nalakuvara and Manigriva began to play in that great holy river. Chikridatuh. With young women. Like two elephant kings sporting with many she-elephants. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.10.4] By telling their greatness, Shukadeva is telling their offense. To tell the greatness is itself the offense. Sporting in the water is pointing to this extreme offense. Sridhara Swami says, a work should be done where it is fitting. Kailasa and the Ganga are not for the sporting of man and woman. From time without beginning, sages have bathed and worshiped in the Ganga. On Kailasa, Shiva himself stays in worship. Even in this age, if a person wandered like this, he would at least fear that someone might see. These two had no fear. They had drunk so much that the mind was not working.

23
01:23:06,370 --> 01:25:45,190
By chance, suddenly, the devarshi, the Lord Narada, arrived there. [Srimad-Bhagavatam 10.10.5] He was coming from somewhere and appeared unexpectedly. Otherwise he used to come to Kailasa. He would hear katha from the lotus mouth of Lord Shiva. Generally Narada, the sage of the demigods, keeps company with Shiva. That day, by yadriccha, was the fortunate day of Nalakuvara and Manigriva. Vishvanatha Chakravarti Thakura also says this was their very great fortune. Yadriccha. There must have been some pious act of a former birth. For the devarshi the word Bhagavan is used. Jiva Goswami and Sanatana Goswami take devarshi to mean that he has the power to govern, the power to curse. It is not that a sadhu walks away in silence. He had the power to bring Nalakuvara and the others under control. The word Bhagavan is speaking his mercy. He was merciful. Therefore, Bhagavan devarshih. How beautifully Shukadeva is speaking. Devarshi Narada is present there, hearing the katha, and Shukadeva is saying this in front of everyone. Apashyat. Narada's glance, full of compassion, fell on the two brothers. Seeing their condition, he understood.

24
01:25:48,620 --> 01:29:52,350
The moment they saw Narada, the women became ashamed. From fear of a curse they came out at once and began to wrap their cloth. They could not dress properly. Somehow they wrapped themselves, moved this way and that, and protected their modesty. There was fear and there was shame. They had heard that sadhus curse, and one has to be burned to ash. Gautama had cursed his wife Ahalya, and she had become stone. They knew that old history. And they knew that bathing naked is an offense. It is a social offense, and it is certainly an offense against scripture. Some people start a path of wandering naked, like animals. Nothing is said to them. But if young men and women wander here and there like this, they will be stopped. One should not bathe naked. Seeing him, the goddesses were ashamed. They were women of heaven. Vridita, ashamed. Being without cloth, they feared a curse. Shanka means fear. They quickly put on their cloth. They saw Narada and recognized him, and at once they came out. The women also signaled, dress, dress. Na va paryadhuh eva. They did not dress. Eva. Srila Sanatana Goswami writes, they should have dressed themselves. The sense did not come from within, so the women were saying again and again, dress, or who knows what Narada will do. Narada is a mahatma famous in the world, famous in the universe. Even an ordinary brahmana's word is enough to make one careful. Such a bad thought came: now the sadhu will not even let us bathe. What need has he to come here. You all dress. We will not dress. Let us see what he does. This was the very path by which he came. He will not even let us bathe. In the end, this was their feeling. This part ends here. The curse is in the katha ahead.
